{"name":"Coyote","hex":"#dc9b68","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Coyote","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["An island of El Salvador.","A trickster figure found in many Native American folklores."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Coyote"}],"entries":[{"title":"Coyote","blocks":[{"text":"The coyote is a species of canine also known as the American jackal, prairie wolf, and brush wolf. It is native to North America, and it is smaller than its close relative, the gray wolf, and slightly smaller than the closely related eastern wolf and red wolf. It fills much of the same ecological niche as the golden jackal does in Eurasia, but the coyote is generally larger."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9c/2009-Coyote-Yosemite.jpg/330px-2009-Coyote-Yosemite.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coyote","wikidata":"Q44299"},{"title":"Coyote (person)","blocks":[{"text":"Colloquially, a coyote is a person who smuggles immigrants across the Mexico–United States border. The word \"coyote\" is a loanword from Mexican Spanish that usually refers to a species of North American canine (Canis latrans)."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/02/US-Mexico_border_fence.jpg/330px-US-Mexico_border_fence.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coyote_(person)","wikidata":"Q5180027"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/44/Coyote_expansion_from_1900_to_2016.gif/500px-Coyote_expansion_from_1900_to_2016.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/44/Coyote_expansion_from_1900_to_2016.gif/1280px-Coyote_expansion_from_1900_to_2016.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https